course: dictionary
lesson: 2044
title: Joining words with conjunctions
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A conjunction is a joining word. It joins words, phrases or whole clauses, so one sentence can say more. «I was tired but I kept going.» The word but joins two ideas and shows they contrast: you were tired, yet you kept going.
explain: Each conjunction tells the reader how two parts relate. And adds, but contrasts, or gives a choice, so shows a result, and because gives a reason. Other common ones are when, if, although and while.
explain: Grammar lessons sort them into two kinds. Coordinating conjunctions (for, and, nor, but, or, yet, so, often remembered as FANBOYS) join equal parts. Subordinating conjunctions (because, when, if, although, while, since, until) start a subordinate clause, which cannot stand alone as a sentence.
fact: A conjunction is a joining word.
fact: Coordinating conjunctions join two main clauses, like «I was tired but I kept going.»
fact: Subordinating conjunctions start a subordinate clause, like «I smiled because I was happy.»
fact: «In conjunction with» means together with, as in «a film made in conjunction with a school».
fact: In space, a conjunction is when two planets, or a planet and the Moon, look very close together in the sky.
exercise: «I was tired but I kept going.» Which word joins the two ideas? | tired | kept | I | *but
exercise: Which word is a subordinating conjunction? | so | or | and | *because
exercise: What does «in conjunction with» mean? | Far away from | Instead of | Before the start of | *Together with
test: «We stayed inside because it was raining.» Which word is the conjunction? | *because | inside | stayed | raining
test: Which conjunction shows a choice between two things? | and | when | so | *or
exam: Which word is a coordinating conjunction? | While | Although | *Yet | Until
